T5  Section 4 by brute force over all top-level inputs
    Definition 4.1, N = 7: a question answered 0 removes at most one member, a question answered 1 removes every member but the human: True
    Proposition 4.6(a): a trap's run has L - i + 1 answers 1 when its index first occurs at position i (6 sizes, all inputs); Pr[at least L/4 answers 1] = 1-(1-1/N)^m0; smallest ratio to (9/32)L/n over n <= 300, L <= n: 1.1137
    Corollary 4.4(b), (N, L) in [(6, 5), (4, 6)], one replaced answer allowed: the corrupted run of the human system has fewer than L/4 answers that differ from the human's at their inputs: True; a member g_a other than the human has at least L - j + 1 unfaithful calls when a first occurs at position j: True
    Corollary 4.4(c), N = 8, L = 5, G = {fewer than L/4 answers 1}: a member fixed before the input is outside G with probability 0.4138 = 1-(1-1/N)^m0; the rule that sees z and deploys the trap of its first symbol is outside G with probability 1.0
    Proposition 4.3(b) for the posterior after any at most n questions (uniform on a set of size |S| >= n that contains the human; n = 16..64, L <= n, all m): largest Pr[more than m unfaithful calls]/bound = 0.7097
    Remark 4.5: the vote's unfaithful calls are Binomial(L, 1/N); largest ratio to the committee's bound = 0.3316
    Corollary 4.4(b): (3/2)^3/3! = 0.5625 <= 0.57
